    <h1>About Dnaso</h1>
    <p>Dnaso is an independent publishing project focused on one thing: <strong>home backup power, explained plainly</strong>.</p>
    <p>When the grid goes down — hurricanes, ice storms, heat waves, or just an aging grid — American homeowners face a confusing market: portable vs standby vs inverter generators, whole-house batteries, transfer switches, fuel types, sizing math, and quotes that range from $500 to $15,000 for what looks like the same job.</p>
    <p>Our guides are written to cut through that confusion with specific numbers, honest comparisons, and practical checklists. We don't sell generators, we don't take commissions from installers, and our content is informational — not a substitute for a licensed electrician's advice on your specific home.</p>
    <h2>How we make money</h2>
    <p>Dnaso is supported by display advertising (Google AdSense). We don't publish sponsored content disguised as guides, and advertisers don't influence what we write.</p>
    <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every guide is researched from manufacturer specs, electrician guidance, and current US pricing.</li>
      <li>We update cost figures and tax-credit information as they change.</li>
      <li>Where safety is involved, we say so clearly — and tell you when to call a professional.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
